        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Roof decks, rear decks and 25-foot lots</strong></p><p>Most San Francisco houses sit on narrow lots, attached to the neighbors, with the outdoor space on the roof or behind the house. Roof decks in Noe Valley, the Mission, Potrero Hill and Bernal Heights look across the city and the bay. Rear decks in Glen Park, Twin Peaks, Diamond Heights and Russian Hill hang over steep slopes, with a long stair down to the yard. A frameless glass railing keeps those views open instead of boxing them in with posts and pickets.</p><p>Pacific Heights, Sea Cliff and the Marina have larger homes with bay-view terraces and balconies. Sea Cliff, the Richmond and the Sunset face the ocean through fog. Across the Golden Gate, Sausalito, Mill Valley and Tiburon hillside homes follow the same deck-and-view pattern. So do Pacifica and Hillsborough down the Peninsula. Inside, open stairs in remodeled Victorians and Edwardians take custom stair glass.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Permits: DBI, Planning and the 42-inch guard</strong></p><p>San Francisco permits go through the Department of Building Inspection (DBI). A new roof deck or an enlarged rear deck usually involves the Planning Department as well, and neighbors may be notified. A railing swap on an existing deck is simpler, but ask DBI before you order; the answer depends on the building and the block.</p><p>The California code sets a 42-inch minimum guard height, including single-family houses (CRC R312.1). That is the height to plan for on a deck or roof deck. Our 39.6-inch panel gives 42 inches; the 46-inch panel gives 48 inches, a sensible choice on a windy roof. The city is seismic, but earthquake rules do not add railing requirements. What matters is the attachment, and our engineering documents cover the spigots on wood and on concrete. Read the <a href=\"/pages/california-building-standards-code-for-glass-railings\">California code page</a> and the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-california\">California hub</a>, then confirm with DBI.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Fog, wind, salt and getting a crate up a hill</strong></p><p>Summer in San Francisco means fog and a steady afternoon wind off the ocean, strongest in the Sunset, the Richmond and Sea Cliff. The fog carries salt, and ordinary steel rusts. Every spigot, connector and hinge we sell is 2205 marine-grade stainless steel with a 10-year no-rust promise, in Brushed Stainless or Matte Black. The 12 mm tempered glass is one solid panel with nothing to rattle. A square caprail is available if you want a continuous top rail on an exposed roof deck.</p><p>Delivery is by freight carrier with liftgate service, 5–10 business days after 2–3 business days of processing. Trucks do not fit every San Francisco street, so tell us if yours is steep or narrow when you order. Plan two people to carry panels up the stairs. Report any damage within 3 days of delivery and before installation for a free replacement or refund.</p>"
          }
        }
